Package: RegCompassR
Title: Multiome Regulatory Evidence and Metabolic Reaction Analysis
Version: 2.4.23
Authors@R: c(person(given = "RegCompass", family = "Developers", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "dev@example.com"))
Author: RegCompass Developers [aut, cre]
Maintainer: RegCompass Developers <dev@example.com>
Description: Integrates paired single-cell RNA and ATAC regulatory evidence
    with genome-scale metabolic models for cell-type-resolved reaction analysis.
    The workflow provides condition-aware or standard Pando regulatory inference,
    multimodal SuperCell aggregation, reaction meta-modules, context-specific
    structural reconstruction, directional metabolic scoring, and post-analysis
    utilities. Stage 1 uses one parallel level at a time and memory-trimmed Pando
    working objects. Condition GRNs preserve Pando regulatory-domain, motif,
    peak-target-correlation and TF-target-correlation candidate logic. Candidate
    discovery is run on pooled eligible-condition cells and independently within
    each condition; exact target-TF-region triples passing the configured gates
    in either scope are deduplicated into one frozen common dictionary. Every
    condition uses this identical ordered dictionary and equal-condition
    within-condition RMS predictor scaling. Conditional production coefficients
    are estimated jointly with Pando E-star at the fixed deviation threshold
    z=0.25. The shared component is the unpenalized joint MLE; only identifiable
    condition deviations receive the information-scaled sparse penalty, while
    zero-information contrasts are fixed to sharing as an explicit boundary
    convention. Formal edge inference is intentionally separated from this
    selected production structure: each condition is fit by a no-fusion Gaussian
    linear model on the same frozen target dictionary, non-estimable coefficients
    remain NA, and each exact edge receives one across-condition omnibus test.
    A single estimable condition retains its finite-residual-df Student t P value;
    multiple estimable conditions use the corresponding Wald chi-square statistic.
    Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment is performed once across all estimable exact
    edges in the broad-cell-type network. An edge enters the common RegCompass
    topology only when this edge-level adjusted P value is below the configured
    threshold and every condition has a valid continuous production coefficient;
    admitted edges are retained in every condition with that condition's own
    E-star coefficient. E-star fusion and boundary fields remain production-model
    diagnostics and are not significance selectors. Full-data target R-squared
    remains diagnostic on the conditional route and is not a hard handoff gate.
    The production condition route exposes no alternative z grid, conditional
    ridge CV, fusion-ratio control, or sensitivity-selection API. Standard Pando
    remains an independent route and keeps its own ridge controls and
    BH/R-squared filtering. Human standard and condition routes share the
    RegCompass default regulatory prior formed by the union of Pando's
    phastConsElements20Mammals.UCSC.hg38 and SCREEN.ccRE.UCSC.hg38 resources.
    Layer 1 combines each admitted condition's continuous E-star coefficient
    with the existing RegCompass paired-cell regulatory exposure: 0.75 times the
    metacell mean of TF-by-ATAC plus 0.25 times the product of separate metacell
    TF and ATAC means. The exposure weight 0.25 is independent of the E-star
    z=0.25 deviation threshold. Stage 2 builds one multimodal WNN graph and one
    Walktrap hierarchy per broad cell type and selects condition-specific feasible
    cuts from that same hierarchy with hard minimum metacell size and count
    constraints. Quantitative Layer 1 RNA is the equal-weight mean of single-cell
    linear CPM within the exact final SuperCell membership. Medium concentrations
    are provenance rather than implicit kinetics, and full-GEM medium cache
    identity is based on the final resolved reaction lower and upper bounds.
    Pando and SuperCell compatibility is checked from required runtime APIs and
    data contracts rather than package version strings; the Remotes entries
    target the current repository default branches. Mathematical and quantitative
    definitions are maintained in the package mathematical specification rather
    than duplicated in interface text.
